In Red Hat Enterprise Linux 9 (RHEL 9), a Virtual Console (also called a virtual terminal or tty) is a full-screen, text-based command-line interface (CLI) that runs directly on the Linux kernel without requiring a graphical user interface (GUI).Here is a comprehensive guide structured specifically for an interview preparation context.💡 Core Concept & ArchitectureDirect Kernel Access: It provides a direct login shell via the kernel device drivers (/dev/tty1 through /dev/tty6).Subsystem Reliance: It operates independently of the X11 or Wayland display servers.Default Allocation: RHEL 9 allocates 6 virtual consoles by default.Systemd Management: The systemd-logind service dynamically manages these sessions, launching a getty process when a console is activated.🔄 Navigation ShortcutsInterviewer-tested shortcuts for switching between consoles in RHEL 9:Ctrl + Alt + F1: Displays the graphical login screen or the GUI desktop session.Ctrl + Alt + F2 through F6: Opens text-based virtual consoles (tty2 to tty6).Chvt <number>: Command-line instruction used to switch consoles programmatically (e.g., chvt 3).

